        <article aria-labelledby="who-we-are">
          <h2 id="who-we-are">Who we are and how remote delivery works for UAE clients</h2>
          <p>
            BtechWaleTech is three engineers based in India. Ankur Kumar is a full stack developer who builds the front ends,
            back ends and integrations. Santosh Sharma works on AI and machine learning, AWS, data, technical SEO and
            deployment. Vedansh Shrivastava runs project management and works on data science, AI and automation. When a
            UAE client asks why a page is slow, how an enquiry reaches their CRM or what happens when a payment fails, the
            answer comes from the person who built that part, not from an account manager relaying a message.
          </p>
          <p>
            We are open about what we are not. We do not have an office in Dubai Internet City or anywhere else in the
            Emirates, we do not hold a UAE trade licence, and we do not have staff who can visit your premises. We have not
            won awards we can point to and we will not quote review scores or client logos on this page. What we can offer
            is a clear process, published starting prices, and a time zone that sits only ninety minutes away from yours.
          </p>
          <h3>What remote delivery looks like day to day</h3>
          <p>
            Most UAE businesses already run a good part of their operations remotely. Suppliers sit in China, accountants
            file from another emirate, and marketing freelancers work from Cairo, Beirut or Karachi. Working with us fits that
            pattern. A project starts with a WhatsApp conversation or a video call on Google Meet, Zoom or Teams. We follow
            up with a written scope, so there is a document both sides can point to later. During the build you get a
            private staging link, which means you can review progress on your phone between meetings rather than waiting for
            a formal presentation.
          </p>
          <p>
            Communication happens in English. For Arabic content we work with text that you or your translator provide,
            and we are careful to say so upfront. Decisions are written down in the same thread where they were agreed, so
            nothing depends on someone remembering a phone call. When we need something from you, such as logo files, photos,
            product data or access to a domain registrar, we ask for it in one consolidated list rather than drip-feeding
            requests over several weeks.
          </p>
          <h3>End to end, including hosting and deployment</h3>
          <p>
            We take a project from planning through design, development, SEO setup, hosting and deployment. You do not need
            a second provider to put the site live, point the domain, install SSL certificates or set up email records so
            your messages stop landing in spam. After launch, five months of maintenance are included free: content updates,
            bug fixes, security updates, backups, and speed and uptime checks. Problems on a new site tend to surface in the
            first few months, which is exactly when that cover matters.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article aria-labelledby="uae-market">
          <h2 id="uae-market">What UAE customers expect from a business website</h2>
          <p>
            The UAE is an unusually mixed audience. Emiratis, long-term expatriate residents from dozens of countries,
            short-term workers, tourists and business visitors all search for the same services, often on a phone and often
            in a hurry. A single website has to work for a Filipino nurse looking for a clinic in Deira, an Indian family
            comparing schools in Sharjah, a European investor researching off-plan property and an Emirati customer who would
            rather read in Arabic. That is why clarity beats cleverness. Clear service names, visible prices or price
            ranges, a map, opening hours and a WhatsApp button do more for enquiries than an elaborate animation.
          </p>
          <p>
            WhatsApp deserves a special mention. In the UAE it is often the first thing a customer reaches for, ahead of a
            contact form or an email. A site that hides its WhatsApp number in the footer loses enquiries. We place a
            click-to-chat link where it is easy to find, pre-fill a short message so the customer does not have to type the
            first line, and, where it helps, pass the page they were viewing so your team knows which service they asked
            about. For higher volumes, the WhatsApp Business Platform can send booking confirmations and order updates
            automatically.
          </p>
          <h3>Trust signals matter more when buyers do not know you</h3>
          <p>
            Much of the audience is new to the country or visiting, so they cannot rely on word of mouth. They judge a
            business by details: a real address and a location pin, a trade name that matches the licence, photos of the
            actual premises rather than stock images, the names and qualifications of the people who will serve them, and a
            clear returns or cancellation policy. We build pages that make these details easy to find, and we will not invent
            testimonials or badges for you. If you have genuine reviews on Google, we can link to them.
          </p>
          <p>
            Speed is part of trust too. UAE mobile networks are fast, but a page stuffed with uncompressed hero videos,
            half a dozen chat widgets and several tracking scripts still feels sluggish. We build light pages, compress
            images into modern formats, load scripts only where they are needed, and check Core Web Vitals before launch so
            the site feels quick on an ordinary phone, not just on a developer's laptop.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article aria-labelledby="arabic-rtl">
          <h2 id="arabic-rtl">Bilingual Arabic and English websites with proper RTL</h2>
          <p>
            English is the working language of most UAE business, but Arabic matters. It is the official language, it is
            expected for government-facing work and many public-sector suppliers, and it builds credibility with Emirati
            customers and with visitors from across the Arab world. An Arabic version also opens up search demand that
            English pages simply do not reach: people searching for <span lang="ar" dir="rtl">تصميم مواقع</span> (website
            design) or <span lang="ar" dir="rtl">عيادة أسنان في دبي</span> (dental clinic in Dubai) are not typing the English
            phrase.
          </p>
          <p>
            The common failure is an Arabic version that was bolted on at the end. The text was translated, a single rule
            flipped the page direction, and everything else was left alone. Arrows point backwards, the logo sits on the wrong
            side, form labels drift away from their fields, and English brand names or phone numbers scramble in the middle
            of Arabic sentences. Visitors notice immediately, and it signals that Arabic readers were an afterthought.
          </p>
          <h3>How we build the Arabic side</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>The page declares <code>lang="ar"</code> and <code>dir="rtl"</code> at the document level, so browsers, screen readers and search engines all know what they are reading.</li>
            <li>Layouts use CSS logical properties such as inline start and end instead of fixed left and right, so one stylesheet serves both directions without a pile of overrides.</li>
            <li>We pick an Arabic typeface that pairs with your English font and has proper weights, then set a larger line height, because Arabic script needs more vertical room to stay readable.</li>
            <li>Letter spacing and uppercase transforms are removed for Arabic text, since they break the joined letterforms.</li>
            <li>Directional icons such as arrows, chevrons and progress steps are mirrored, while logos, media controls and brand marks are not.</li>
            <li>Phone numbers, prices, product codes and English brand names inside Arabic text are isolated so they display in the correct order.</li>
            <li>Forms, validation messages, date pickers and email notifications are tested in Arabic, not only the page content.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            We also make a deliberate choice about numerals. Many UAE sites use Western digits in both languages because
            that is what customers see on price tags and phone numbers, while some formal sites prefer Eastern Arabic digits
            such as <span lang="ar" dir="rtl">١٢٣</span> in Arabic text. Either is fine; mixing them randomly is not. We agree
            the rule with you once and apply it everywhere, including in the checkout and invoices.
          </p>
          <h3>Who writes the Arabic</h3>
          <p>
            We are honest about this: nobody on our team is a native Arabic copywriter, and machine translation is not good
            enough for a customer-facing site in a market where readers are sensitive to tone. You supply the Arabic text,
            or a translator you trust does. To make that easier, we export every string that needs translating into a single
            sheet, including button labels, error messages, page titles and meta descriptions, so nothing is left in English
            by accident. If you only need English today, we still structure the site so an Arabic version can be added later
            without a rebuild.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article aria-labelledby="ecommerce">
          <h2 id="ecommerce">Ecommerce and payments for UAE online stores</h2>
          <p>
            Ecommerce builds start from {usd("Ecommerce")}. The platform matters less than people think; the details of
            payment, delivery and tax matter more. A UAE store has to show prices in dirhams with VAT handled correctly,
            offer the payment methods local shoppers trust, promise delivery times it can actually meet, and let customers
            reach a person quickly when something goes wrong. Big marketplaces such as Amazon and noon have trained shoppers
            to expect fast delivery and simple returns, so an independent store needs to be clear and dependable to compete.
          </p>
          <h3>Payment gateways we integrate</h3>
          <p>
            We integrate whichever gateway your company signs up with. Common choices for UAE businesses include Stripe,
            Checkout.com, Telr, PayTabs and Network International. Each differs in onboarding requirements, settlement times,
            supported currencies and fees, and each will ask for your trade licence and bank details during approval. Apple
            Pay and Google Pay can usually be switched on through the gateway and are popular on mobile. We test the full
            flow on staging, including declined cards, 3D Secure challenges, refunds and partial refunds, and card details
            never touch your own server.
          </p>
          <div class="comparison-table-wrap table-in-prose">
            <table class="price-table fit-sm">
              <caption class="sr-only">Payment options for UAE online stores</caption>
              <thead><tr><th scope="col">Option</th><th scope="col">Where it fits</th><th scope="col" class="hide-sm">What to plan for</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><th scope="row">Card gateways (Stripe, Checkout.com, Telr, PayTabs, Network International)</th><td>Core online payments by card, Apple Pay and Google Pay</td><td class="hide-sm">Merchant approval with your licence, fees, settlement time, refund workflow</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Tabby and Tamara</th><td>Buy now, pay later on higher-value baskets such as fashion, electronics and furniture</td><td class="hide-sm">Provider approval, minimum and maximum order values, how returns are refunded</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Cash on delivery</th><td>First-time buyers and categories where trust is still being built</td><td class="hide-sm">Order confirmation by WhatsApp or call, courier cash collection, failed delivery handling</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Bank transfer and payment links</th><td>B2B orders, custom quotes and large invoices</td><td class="hide-sm">Manual reconciliation, clear order status until payment clears</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <h3>Buy now, pay later</h3>
          <p>
            Tabby and Tamara are widely recognised by UAE shoppers, and showing an instalment option on product pages can
            make a higher price feel manageable. Both have their own approval process and rules about which products and
            order values qualify. We add their checkout option and on-page messaging according to each provider's guidelines,
            and we make sure returns and partial refunds flow back correctly, which is where many stores get into trouble.
          </p>
          <h3>Shopify, WooCommerce or custom</h3>
          <p>
            Shopify is the right answer for most small and mid-sized UAE stores. It handles hosting, security and updates,
            supports Arabic storefront themes, and works with the major gateways and BNPL providers through apps.
            WooCommerce suits businesses that want a content-heavy site, such as a brand with a blog, recipes or guides,
            with a store inside it. A custom build makes sense when pricing is unusual, when you sell B2B with customer-specific
            price lists, or when the store has to talk to an ERP in real time. We recommend one after looking at your
            catalogue, order volume and the people who will run the store.
          </p>
          <h3>Delivery, catalogue and checkout details</h3>
          <p>
            Delivery promises need to be specific. Same-day or next-day delivery within Dubai is common, while the northern
            emirates, Al Ain and remote areas may take longer. We build shipping rules by emirate or area into the checkout
            so customers see the real cost and timing before paying, rather than a vague note asking them to call. Couriers
            such as Aramex and Emirates Post, and local same-day services, can be connected so tracking links reach the
            customer automatically.
          </p>
          <p>
            On the catalogue side, we care about product schema so listings can show price and availability in search, filters
            that do not create thousands of junk URLs for Google to crawl, size guides in the units your customers use, and
            product names that work in both languages. Abandoned cart emails, a clear returns page with actual working days,
            and a WhatsApp support link on every product page all reduce the number of customers who give up at the last step.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article aria-labelledby="seo">
          <h2 id="seo">SEO for google.ae in English and Arabic</h2>
          <p>
            SEO setup is part of every build: clean URLs, unique titles and descriptions, a logical heading structure,
            internal links, compressed images, schema markup for your business and services, an XML sitemap and a sensible
            robots file. Search Console and analytics are connected before launch so you have data from day one. For ongoing
            work, monthly SEO starts from {usd("Monthly SEO")}. The <a href="/services/seo-services/">SEO services page</a>
            explains what that covers in general; here is what changes for the UAE.
          </p>
          <h3>Arabic keyword research is its own project</h3>
          <p>
            Translating an English keyword list into Arabic does not work. Arabic searchers use different words, different
            word order and a mix of Modern Standard Arabic and Gulf dialect. Some search in Arabic script, some in English,
            and some in Latin-letter Arabic. The same need might be searched as <span lang="ar" dir="rtl">شركة تطوير مواقع</span>,
            <span lang="ar" dir="rtl">برمجة مواقع</span> or <span lang="ar" dir="rtl">تصميم متجر إلكتروني</span> depending on
            what the person has in mind. We research each language separately using search data, autocomplete and the pages
            that already rank on google.ae, then map keywords to pages so the Arabic page is written for Arabic searchers, not
            just translated from English.
          </p>
          <h3>hreflang and URL structure</h3>
          <p>
            Each language lives on its own URL, typically a folder such as <code>/ar/</code> for Arabic and the root or
            <code>/en/</code> for English. We add hreflang annotations so Google shows the Arabic page to Arabic searchers and
            the English page to English searchers, with region codes such as ar-AE and en-AE if you also target other Gulf
            countries. We never swap languages with a script on a single URL, because search engines then only see one
            version. Titles, meta descriptions and structured data are written per language.
          </p>
          <h3>Local and emirate-level search</h3>
          <p>
            A lot of UAE searches are local: a clinic near Al Barsha, a plumber in Khalifa City, a car repair garage in
            Industrial Area Sharjah. We plan pages around the emirates and areas you genuinely serve, with content specific
            to each, rather than cloning one paragraph and swapping the place name. We also help you set up or clean up your
            Google Business Profile, keep your name, address and phone number consistent across listings, and add LocalBusiness
            schema. Maps visibility often matters more than organic rankings for walk-in businesses.
          </p>
          <h3>Competition is intense, so focus wins</h3>
          <p>
            Dubai search results for property, travel, cars and financial services are dominated by portals, aggregators,
            paid ads and long-established brands. Trying to rank for a broad term like "apartments Dubai" is usually a waste of
            budget for a small firm. Narrower pages built around a specific service, area and buyer, with real detail on
            price ranges, process and timelines, have a far better chance. We will tell you plainly when monthly SEO is worth
            it and when a well-built site plus a good Google Business Profile is enough.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article aria-labelledby="software">
          <h2 id="software">Custom software and AI automation for UAE operations</h2>
          <p>
            Many UAE businesses run on a mix of spreadsheets, WhatsApp groups and email threads that worked when the company
            was small and now cost hours every week. That is where custom software and automation earn their keep. A custom
            web app starts from {usd("Custom web app")} and AI automation from {usd("AI automation")}; both are scoped against
            the time and errors they remove, not against a wish list.
          </p>
          <h3>Typical UAE use cases</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>A broker portal where agents add listings once and they appear on your site, with enquiries routed to the right agent by area.</li>
            <li>A booking and scheduling system for a clinic, salon or car service centre, with reminders sent over WhatsApp.</li>
            <li>A dealer or distributor portal for a trading company, showing customer-specific prices, stock levels and order history.</li>
            <li>An operations dashboard pulling orders, deliveries and payments into one view for a founder who is tired of asking for reports.</li>
            <li>AI extraction of data from supplier invoices, bills of lading and delivery notes, so staff check figures instead of retyping them.</li>
            <li>An assistant that answers routine customer questions in English and Arabic from your own documents, and hands anything unusual to a person.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            We start by mapping the current workflow with the people who actually do the work, because the manager's version
            and the reality often differ. Then we build the smallest version that removes the biggest pain, put it in front of
            real users on a staging link, and extend it from there. Integrations are common: Zoho, HubSpot and Salesforce CRMs,
            Google Workspace and Microsoft 365, accounting tools, property portals and courier APIs.
          </p>
          <p>
            We will also tell you when automation is not worth it. If a task happens twice a month, a checklist is cheaper than
            software. If the process itself is unclear, automating it just makes the confusion faster. For AI features, we
            explain where the model can make mistakes, keep a human review step where errors would be costly, and avoid sending
            sensitive personal or health data to third-party AI services without your explicit decision and your adviser's
            agreement.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article aria-labelledby="emirates">
          <h2 id="emirates">Emirate by emirate: what businesses need online</h2>
          <p>
            The seven emirates share federal laws but differ a lot in economy, audience and competition. We serve all of
            them remotely. A short summary is in the emirates table further down the page; here is more context on how
            each market shapes a website.
          </p>
          <h3>Dubai</h3>
          <p>
            Dubai is the country's commercial centre and its most competitive online market. Real estate, tourism, retail,
            trading, logistics through Jebel Ali and a dense layer of professional services all compete for attention in the
            same search results. Websites here need to be fast, bilingual where the audience warrants it, and very clear
            about location, because a customer in Dubai Marina will not drive to Deira for a routine service. Area-level
            pages, strong Google Business Profiles and WhatsApp-first contact work well.
          </p>
          <h3>Abu Dhabi and Al Ain</h3>
          <p>
            Abu Dhabi, the capital, has a more formal business culture shaped by government, energy, healthcare, education
            and culture. Companies supplying government entities or large groups often need a polished bilingual corporate
            site with a downloadable company profile, clear certifications and leadership pages. Al Ain, inland near the
            Omani border, behaves like its own market with a more local, family-oriented customer base, so we treat it as a
            separate target rather than a footnote to the capital.
          </p>
          <h3>Sharjah</h3>
          <p>
            Sharjah combines manufacturing and industrial areas, many schools and universities, a large residential
            population that commutes to Dubai, and a strong cultural identity. Manufacturers and traders need clear
            catalogue sites with specification downloads and enquiry forms their sales team can work from. Education
            providers need admissions information that answers parents' questions without a phone call. Sharjah also
            includes east coast towns such as Khor Fakkan and Kalba, which are different markets again.
          </p>
          <h3>Ajman and Umm Al Quwain</h3>
          <p>
            Ajman and Umm Al Quwain are smaller emirates with many small traders, clinics, workshops and property firms,
            plus free zones that attract startups because of lower setup costs. Budgets are tighter, so a fast static site
            starting from {usd("Static")}, a strong Google Business Profile and a clear WhatsApp link usually deliver more
            value than a large custom build. We keep these projects lean.
          </p>
          <h3>Ras Al Khaimah</h3>
          <p>
            Ras Al Khaimah has grown into a tourism and resort destination alongside its industrial base in ceramics, cement
            and manufacturing, and its economic zone hosts many international companies. Hospitality businesses need
            photo-rich pages that still load quickly and a direct booking path. Industrial firms need export-ready catalogue
            sites. Companies registered in the economic zone but trading worldwide often need an international-facing
            corporate site rather than a local one.
          </p>
          <h3>Fujairah</h3>
          <p>
            Fujairah, on the east coast, is centred on its port, shipping, bunkering and storage industries, with tourism
            around its beaches, mountains and diving sites. Marine and shipping service companies need credible B2B sites
            that explain services, certifications and contact routes clearly for an international audience. Tourism businesses
            need seasonal offer pages and booking forms that work well on mobile.
          </p>
          <h3>Free zones</h3>
          <p>
            Free zone companies, from DMCC trading houses and Dubai Internet City tech firms to DIFC and ADGM financial
            businesses and companies in SAIF Zone or RAKEZ, often sell far beyond the UAE. Their websites need to speak to
            international buyers, investors and partners as much as to local customers. Financial free zones also have their
            own data protection regimes, covered in the next section, which affects how forms and analytics are set up.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article aria-labelledby="compliance">
          <h2 id="compliance">Data protection, VAT and rules that affect UAE websites</h2>
          <p>
            A short but important disclaimer first: we are engineers, not lawyers or tax advisers. What follows is a general
            description of rules that often come up when we build UAE websites, so you know what to ask your legal and tax
            advisers. It is not legal advice, and rules change, so always confirm the current position for your company.
          </p>
          <h3>Federal Personal Data Protection Law</h3>
          <p>
            The UAE issued a federal Personal Data Protection Law (PDPL) in 2021. In broad terms it sets expectations around
            having a lawful basis, usually consent, for processing personal data, telling people how their data will be used,
            keeping data secure, and respecting individuals' rights over their data. It also addresses sending data outside
            the country. Check with your adviser how it applies to your company and the current status of its implementing
            regulations. On the technical side, we build forms that collect only the fields you need, show a plain-language
            notice near the submit button, record consent where needed, and link to a privacy policy page.
          </p>
          <h3>DIFC and ADGM</h3>
          <p>
            Companies in the Dubai International Financial Centre and Abu Dhabi Global Market fall under their own data
            protection laws and regulators, which are modelled closely on international standards. If your company sits in
            one of these free zones, your privacy notice, cookie approach and any data sent to overseas service providers,
            including analytics and email tools, should follow those rules. We set up cookie consent and analytics
            configuration to match whatever approach your adviser recommends.
          </p>
          <h3>Health and sector rules</h3>
          <p>
            Some sectors have extra requirements. Health information is treated with particular care in the UAE, and there are
            rules limiting where certain health data may be stored and processed. For clinics, we keep website forms to
            appointment requests, avoid collecting medical details through the site, and can deploy on UAE-region cloud
            hosting if your adviser or regulator requires it. Financial services, education and government suppliers can
            also have sector-specific expectations worth checking.
          </p>
          <h3>VAT and e-invoicing</h3>
          <p>
            The UAE has charged VAT at a standard rate of 5 percent since 2018. For your online store, that usually means
            showing consumer prices inclusive of VAT, issuing tax invoices with your tax registration number, and handling
            VAT correctly on shipping and discounts. We configure the store to match what your accountant specifies. The UAE
            has also announced a mandatory e-invoicing programme that is being phased in for businesses; if your store or
            billing system will need to connect to an accredited e-invoicing provider, tell us early so we plan the
            integration. Confirm the dates and requirements that apply to you with your accountant.
          </p>
          <h3>Consumer protection and licensing</h3>
          <p>
            UAE consumer protection rules expect clear pricing, honest descriptions and transparent terms for returns and
            refunds. Selling online also generally needs the right activity on your trade licence, which is a matter for your
            licensing authority, not your web developer. We make sure the site shows your legal trade name, contact details,
            terms of sale and returns policy clearly, and we avoid fake urgency timers and pre-ticked boxes.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article aria-labelledby="time-zones">
          <h2 id="time-zones">Time zones and the working week</h2>
          <p>
            Gulf Standard Time is four hours ahead of UTC and India Standard Time is five and a half hours ahead, and neither
            country uses daylight saving. So the UAE is always exactly one and a half hours behind India. Our working hours of
            9am to 9pm IST run from 7.30am to 7.30pm in the UAE, which covers the whole of a normal Dubai office day with time
            to spare on both ends. This is one of the easiest offshore arrangements there is: no midnight calls and no
            overnight wait for a reply.
          </p>
          <div class="comparison-table-wrap table-in-prose">
            <table class="price-table fit-sm">
              <caption class="sr-only">India and UAE time comparison</caption>
              <thead><tr><th scope="col">UAE time (GST)</th><th scope="col">India time (IST)</th><th scope="col">What usually happens</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><th scope="row">7.30am</th><td>9.00am</td><td>We start; overnight WhatsApp messages answered</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">9.00am</th><td>10.30am</td><td>Your office opens; good time for a quick call</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">1.00pm</th><td>2.30pm</td><td>Midday check-in or review of the staging link</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">5.00pm</th><td>6.30pm</td><td>End-of-day update with what changed today</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">7.30pm</th><td>9.00pm</td><td>Our day ends; urgent issues still reach us on WhatsApp</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <h3>Weekends</h3>
          <p>
            Since 2022, UAE federal government bodies have worked Monday to Friday with a shorter Friday and a Saturday and
            Sunday weekend, and many private companies have followed. Some businesses still close on Friday, and Sharjah's
            government follows a longer weekend. India's working week is generally Monday to Saturday or Monday to Friday. In
            practice the two calendars overlap almost completely, and we reply on WhatsApp seven days a week. We schedule
            launches and major changes early in your working week so there is time to fix anything before your weekend, and
            we plan around Ramadan working hours, Eid holidays and UAE National Day when timing a launch.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article aria-labelledby="payments">
          <h2 id="payments">How UAE clients pay us</h2>
          <p>
            We quote and invoice in US dollars by default, because our international pricing is set in dollars. If your
            finance team prefers dirhams, we can invoice in AED on request. The dirham has been pegged to the dollar for
            decades, so the conversion is predictable and the amount does not drift between quote and payment. Payment is by
            international bank wire or Wise. We do not ask for card details and we do not use crypto.
          </p>
          <p>
            Payments are split into milestones: a deposit to start work, a payment when you approve the staging site, and the
            balance at launch. Each milestone has a proper invoice showing our details and the scope it covers. Monthly SEO and
            maintenance plans are invoiced monthly in advance and can be stopped with notice. Third-party costs such as your
            domain, hosting, Shopify plan, premium plugins and gateway fees are paid by your company directly to those
            providers, so the accounts stay in your name.
          </p>
          <p>
            Because we are a foreign supplier billing from India, we do not add UAE VAT to our invoices. Whether your company
            needs to self-account for VAT on imported services depends on your registration, so ask your accountant. If you
            need specific wording or a purchase order number on our invoice for your records, tell us before the first
            invoice is issued.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article aria-labelledby="buying-guide">
          <h2 id="buying-guide">A buying guide: hiring an offshore web developer from the UAE</h2>
          <p>
            Many UAE businesses hire developers outside the country, and plenty of those projects go well. The ones that go
            badly usually fail for predictable reasons: an unclear scope, a low bid that grows later, a developer who
            disappears after launch, or logins that were never handed over. Here is how to avoid those problems, whether you
            hire us or someone else.
          </p>
          <h3>Ask for a written scope before you pay</h3>
          <p>
            A proper scope lists the pages, features, integrations, languages and what is not included. It should say who
            supplies content and photos, how many rounds of revisions are included, and what happens if you want to add
            something midway. If a provider will only quote a single number without a scope, the number is not reliable.
          </p>
          <h3>Check who actually does the work</h3>
          <p>
            Ask directly whether the people you speak to will build the site or whether it will be passed to another team or
            subcontractor. There is nothing wrong with a larger team, but you should know who is responsible. With us, the
            three engineers named on this page do the work.
          </p>
          <h3>Look at live work, not just screenshots</h3>
          <p>
            Screenshots and design mockups are easy to borrow. Ask for live links, open them on your phone, and check how
            fast they load and whether forms work. If the provider claims Arabic experience, look at a live Arabic page and
            check whether it is genuinely right to left or just translated text in a left-to-right layout.
          </p>
          <h3>Insist on ownership from day one</h3>
          <p>
            Your domain, hosting, analytics, Search Console, payment gateway and email accounts should be registered in your
            company's name, with the developer given access rather than the other way round. At the end of the project you
            should receive the source code and every login. A website held hostage for renewal fees is one of the most common
            complaints about cheap providers.
          </p>
          <h3>Pay in milestones</h3>
          <p>
            Avoid paying the full amount upfront. Milestones tied to visible progress, such as an approved design, a working
            staging site and the final launch, protect both sides. Use traceable payment methods such as bank transfer or
            Wise and keep invoices for every payment.
          </p>
          <h3>Agree on support after launch</h3>
          <p>
            Ask what happens when something breaks a month after launch. Is there a support period, what does it cover, and
            what does ongoing maintenance cost? Our answer is five months of maintenance included free, then an optional
            monthly plan from {usd("Maintenance")} or pay-as-needed changes.
          </p>
          <h3>Be realistic about SEO promises</h3>
          <p>
            Be wary of anyone guaranteeing first-page rankings in Dubai within a fixed number of weeks. Nobody controls
            Google. A trustworthy provider explains what they will do, how they will report progress and what realistic
            results look like for your competition.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article aria-labelledby="cost">
          <h2 id="cost">What a website really costs for a UAE business</h2>
          <p>
            Prices for websites in the UAE vary enormously, from very cheap template sites to large agency projects. The
            difference is rarely just profit margin; it reflects scope, quality, languages, integrations and who does the
            work. Our starting prices give you a reference point: {usd("Static")} for a static website, {usd("SEO website")}
            for a 700+ page SEO website, {usd("AI automation")} for AI automation, {usd("Ecommerce")} for an ecommerce store
            and {usd("Custom web app")} for a custom web app. Here is what moves a quote above the starting price.
          </p>
          <div class="comparison-table-wrap table-in-prose">
            <table class="price-table fit-sm">
              <caption class="sr-only">Factors that change the cost of a UAE website</caption>
              <thead><tr><th scope="col">Cost factor</th><th scope="col">Why it adds cost</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><th scope="row">Arabic version</th><td>A second layout direction, fonts, testing every template in RTL, and bilingual metadata and hreflang</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Number of unique templates</th><td>Each distinct page design needs building and testing; many pages on one template cost far less</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Integrations</th><td>CRMs, property portals, booking engines, ERPs and couriers each need setup, testing and error handling</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Payments</th><td>Every gateway, BNPL option and cash on delivery flow adds checkout logic and test cases</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Content work</th><td>Writing, editing, product data entry and image preparation take time if we handle them</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Deadlines</th><td>A launch fixed to an event, a trade show or a season may need more hours in a short window</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>
            Running costs matter too. Budget for your domain renewal, hosting or a Shopify plan, any premium plugins or
            apps, gateway transaction fees, WhatsApp Business Platform messaging charges if you use them, and optional
            monthly SEO or maintenance. A static website is usually the cheapest to run because it needs very little server
            power and has fewer moving parts to update. We list expected running costs in the quote so there are no
            surprises after launch.
          </p>
          <p>
            One practical tip: if budget is tight, launch a smaller English site first, structured so Arabic and ecommerce can
            be added later without rebuilding. Many businesses get more value from a fast, clear site live next month than from
            a perfect site six months from now. For current figures and what each package includes, see our
            <a href="/pricing/">pricing page</a>.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

